Day 284/300: Minimum Changes To Make Alternating Binary String

Problem Statement #1758

Problem Statement Explantion:

You are given a string s consisting only of the characters '0' and '1'. In one operation, you can change any '0' to '1' or vice versa.
The string is called alternating if no two adjacent characters are equal. For example, the string "010" is alternating, while the string "0100" is not.
Return the minimum number of operations needed to make the string alternating.

Solution Approach:

An alternating binary string of length n can only follow one of two possible patterns:

Pattern A: Starts with 0 (e.g., 010101...)
Pattern B: Starts with 1 (e.g., 101010...)

Key Insight
The number of operations required to achieve Pattern B is simply n−(operations for Pattern A). 
This is because every character that matches Pattern A will necessarily mismatch Pattern B, and vice versa.

Steps:

Iterate through the string once.
Assume we want to match Pattern A (starts with 0).
For every even index i, the character should be '0'.
For every odd index i, the character should be '1'.
Count how many characters in the input string s do not match this pattern.
The answer is the minimum of count and s.length() - count

Here's the Code:

class Solution {
    public int minOperations(String s) {
        int count0 = 0;
        int n = s.length();
        
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (i % 2 == 0) {
                if (s.charAt(i) != '0') count0++;
            } else {
                if (s.charAt(i) != '1') count0++;
            }
        }
        
        return Math.min(count0, n - count0);
    }
}

Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)



